a='content/os/01-processes/proc-what-os-does.md';b=a.replace('.md','.questions.json')
src=['https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/uio-howto.html','https://docs.sel4.systems/projects/sel4/verified-configurations.html','https://sel4.systems/Verification/assumptions.html','https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu','https://docs.kernel.org/bpf/verifier.html','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/gettingstarted/user-mode-and-kernel-mode']
changes=[('Abstraction is why the same `open`, `read`, `write`, `close` calls work on a file on an NVMe SSD, a USB stick, a pipe, or a network socket. In Unix this idea goes furthest: "everything is a file descriptor".','File descriptors give many resources a common `read`, `write` and `close` interface, though their semantics differ. Regular files are opened with `open`; pipes and sockets normally come from `pipe` and `socket`. Not every resource or operation fits this interface.'),('This program has no idea whether','This minimal illustration omits error handling and partial-write retries; a robust copy must handle both. It has no idea whether'),('Process A literally cannot name process B\'s memory.','An ordinary load in A uses A\'s mappings, not B\'s. Explicit shared mappings and authorised process-memory interfaces are exceptions.'),('Only the kernel can touch device registers, page tables and interrupt settings.','The kernel controls access to device registers, page tables and interrupt settings; it can delegate selected device mappings or I/O permissions to user-space drivers.'),('Linux has around 350–450 of them depending on architecture.','The available syscall set depends on architecture and kernel version.'),('giving a safe way to extend the kernel.','constraining extensions according to the verifier and runtime rules; verifier or kernel bugs can still undermine that protection.'),('seL4\'s core has a machine-checked proof of functional correctness.','seL4 has machine-checked functional-correctness proofs for specified configurations and assumptions, not a proof that every deployment and device is safe.'),('Linux dominates servers, while microkernels dominate where reliability and certification matter most.','Both designs remain in use; their names alone do not establish performance, reliability or certification suitability.'),('| Speed | High | Lower | High |','| Speed | Workload-dependent | IPC-sensitive | Workload-dependent |'),('- The kernel image plus modules is tens of megabytes. The bulk of the Linux source tree is device drivers.','- Kernel image and module sizes depend on configuration, architecture, compression and installed drivers.'),('The kernel runs only when something enters it: a system call, an interrupt or an exception. The rest of the time, user code runs directly on the CPU at full speed.','User code can run directly on the CPU until a system call, interrupt or exception transfers control. Kernel threads also execute scheduled background work; another core can be running kernel code at the same time.'),('## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content gap: deployment assurances\n> No benchmark, market-share survey or certified device design is supplied. Kernel categories alone do not guarantee speed or safety. seL4 guarantees depend on verified configuration and hardware assumptions, including DMA behaviour; fault isolation is not a complete safety case.\n\n## Key takeaways')]
for old,new in changes:edit(a,old,new,'Qualify abstraction, delegated hardware access and proof/deployment guarantees; remove unsupported universal specifics.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q1','prompt','A program uses `read(fd, buf, n)` through the same interface for a regular file, pipe or TCP socket, even though details such as blocking and short reads differ. Which job of the OS does this illustrate?','A shared interface does not give identical resource semantics.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q2','prompt','Why can an ordinary user program without delegated device mappings or I/O permission not directly control the disk controller?','Account for authorised user-space drivers.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q2','explanation','The kernel normally withholds the necessary mappings and I/O permissions. Hardware enforces that policy; selected drivers can receive explicitly delegated access.','Hardware policy permits authorised delegation.',src,option=0)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q2','explanation','Encryption is optional and unrelated to whether the process has authorised device access.','Remove claim that user mode can never drive devices.',src,option=2)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q2','workedExample','An ordinary process lacks device mappings and I/O permissions, so an unauthorised access faults. It normally requests file operations through a syscall. The kernel can deliberately expose selected device memory to a user-space driver, as Linux UIO does; user mode alone does not forbid every device access.','Make delegation exception explicit throughout explanation.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q4','prompt','Suppose a fault in an in-kernel Linux network driver corrupts kernel state, while a comparable fault in an isolated QNX driver process is contained and the service can be restarted. Which design difference explains this?','A wild pointer does not always panic or produce automatic successful recovery.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q4','workedExample','An in-kernel driver shares privileged memory, so its corruption can affect unrelated kernel state. A driver in a separate process has a smaller accessible address space and can be supervised for restart. Actual containment also depends on shared memory, device/DMA permissions and dependencies; restart is not an automatic guarantee of uninterrupted service. IPC and scheduling add costs that depend on the implementation.','Scope fault containment and restart; device authority can cross CPU-memory isolation.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q6','text','Ordinary instructions run directly in user mode; syscalls, exceptions and interrupts enter the kernel, and scheduled kernel threads can perform background work','Include kernel threads and multicore concurrency.',src,option=0)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q6','explanation','The kernel does not interpret every instruction. Hardware enforces protection, while entry events and scheduled kernel work give it CPU time.','Avoid exclusive event-handler model.',src,option=0)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q6','workedExample','On one core, a user thread can execute directly until a syscall, exception or interrupt enters kernel code. The scheduler may then run another user thread or a kernel thread. Different cores can execute user and kernel code concurrently. Timer-based preemption can prevent a normal time-sharing task from monopolising a core; the kernel does not supervise each instruction in software.','Correct kernel-thread omission and scope time-sharing.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q8','explanation','Not the best example as written: address policy may be set by user-space tools, but the kernel also implements mechanisms such as IPv6 autoconfiguration. The cgroup/systemd option is the clearer capability-versus-policy split.','Linux can generate/configure IPv6 addresses; no absolute userspace-only assertion.',src,option=3)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q9','prompt','Why does a small seL4 kernel make whole-kernel functional-correctness verification more tractable than a much larger Linux configuration?','Avoid treating formal verification as fundamentally impossible for Linux.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q9','text','A small kernel limits the implementation and specification that must be covered; Linux configurations can include many more privileged subsystems','Remove unchecked universal source-line counts.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q9','explanation','A smaller kernel can reduce proof scope. The exact guarantee still depends on verified configuration and assumptions; user-space device authority can matter to the trusted system.','Avoid proof scope incorrectly excluding every driver.',src,option=1)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q9','workedExample','seL4 publishes proofs for particular architecture/configuration combinations. A small privileged kernel makes comprehensive reasoning more tractable, while a larger implementation and changing specification increase effort. This does not prove every device deployment safe: DMA, hardware behaviour, boot assumptions and delegated authority must satisfy the documented proof assumptions.','Correct absolute isolation and unsupported line-count/release metrics.',src)
qe(b,'proc-what-os-does-q11','workedExample','A small trusted kernel and isolated services can reduce the scope of some assurance arguments and contain some faults. A device safety case still includes application behaviour, deadlines, recovery, hardware and driver/DMA authority. No supplied implementation, hazard analysis or certification evidence establishes whether Linux, QNX or seL4 is suitable for this particular pump, or whether IPC overhead is acceptable.','Remove unsupported medical-device suitability and performance guarantee.',src)
save([a,b])
